.TH SHUTDOWN\-TRIGGER 1 "" "" ""
.SH NAME

.TP 20
shutdown-trigger \-  gently shutdown a computer powered by a UPS upon a
power failure
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBshutdown-trigger\fP [\fBdaemon options\fP]
.br
\fBshutdown-trigger -b\fP [\fBclient options\fP]

.br
.SH DESCRIPTION

\fBshutdown-trigger\fP is a bash script which will gently shutdown
your server connected to an Uninterruptible Power Supply (UPS) in case
of a power failure.

If the UPS has hardware and software ability to communicate with the
connected devices (for example via a usb cable), then you don't need
\fBshutdown-trigger\fP.

But if the UPS has no support for communication and the main power
failure lasts for long enough (and you are not home or you are deeply
sleeping), then chances are that it will exhaust its batteries and
your server will be switched off the brutal way. This is the scenario
where \fBshutdown-trigger\fP can be useful.

\fBshutdown-trigger\fP will detect a power failure condition and
immediately (and gently) shutdown your server(s) while they are still
powered by the batteries of the UPS.

Power failure detection is obtained by polling an external device
(call it "trigger device") which must satisfy the following two
requirements:

.nr step 1 1
.IP \n[step] 2
is powered by the main line (that is: it is NOT connected to the UPS)
.IP \n+[step]
is connected to the server (typically at a USB port) and its
connection can be polled by the server

The hardware layout is:

.nf 
    +--------------------------------------------------+
    |              MAIN POWER LINE                     |
    +-------+-----------------------------+------------+
            |                             |
            |                             |
            |                        +----+-----+
            |                        |   UPS    |
            |                        +----+-----+
            |                             |              
            |                             |              
            |                             |              
   +--------+---------+    USB       +----+-------------+
   |  trigger device  +--------------+  server running  |
   +------------------+              | shutdown-trigger |
                                     +------------------+

.fi

\fBshutdown-trigger\fP runs as a daemon and periodically checks
(typically with a period of tens of seconds) for the connection of the
trigger device. If there is a power failure, the trigger device will
die and the check for its connection will fail: this is how the daemon
knows that it's time to shutdown the server.

The trigger device can be any cheap device which can be brutally
switched off (by cutting its power supply) without damage and supports
connection (typically via a USB cable) to a computer.

\fBshutdown-trigger\fP has support for shutting down not only the server
which it is running on, but also any other computer reachable from the
server on the same network. This is useful when you have multiple
servers powered by the UPS.


.SH COMMAND LINE OPTIONS

\fBshutdown-trigger\fP is run as a daemon. A \fBshutdown-trigger\fP
daemon running in the background can be contacted by a second instance
of \fBshutdown-trigger\fP run in the foreground as a client.

The communication between the client and the daemon is implemented by
using the \fBncat\fP command of the \fBnmap\fP package
(\fBhttps://nmap.org/\fP), which, for this reason, is a mandatory
dependency.

Some command line options are shared by the daemon and the client,
other options only apply either to the daemon or the client.

At start time, \fBshutdown-trigger\fP reads a configuration file
documented in \fBshutdown-trigger.conf(5)\fP. Command line options
override definitions in the configuration file.

.SS DAEMON OPTIONS

.TP
\fB-C\fP
dump to stdout the current configuration, as resulting from the
command line options and configuration file, and exit
.TP
\fB-l LOG_FILE\fP
the absolute path to the log file.
.br
During normal operation, the daemon uses LOG_FILE for both
periodically logging heartbeat characters (see below) and printing
event driven messages (e.g. advertising a power failure).
.br
Default log file is /var/log/shutdown-trigger.log
.TP
\fB-f\fP
when run as a daemon, \fBshutdown-trigger\fP immediately re-runs
itself in the background. If the \fB-f\fP option is given on the
command line, it will stay in the foreground. This can be useful for
debugging.
.TP
\fB-P N\fP
communication between a running daemon and a client
is implemented through the \fBncat(1)\fP command. The daemon spawns a
daemonized ncat listening on port \fBN\fP. The client sends messages
to the daemon by running another instance of ncat on the same port.
.br
The default value for this option is -1: this value tells the daemon
to pick the first available port >= 38000. Any value >0 will force the
daemon to use the port with that value.
.TP
\fB-s S\fP
sleep S seconds between successive
polls of the trigger device
.br
Default: 30s

.P
The following options control heartbeat logging. The daemon dumps
to LOG_FILE heartbeat characters every S seconds. The structure and
meaning of this heartbeat logging is best explained with an example:

.nf
STARTING
2026-10-09T16:38:22-01:00
   1 .........|.........|.........|.........|.........|.........|.........
  70 .........|.........|.........|.........|.........|.........|.........
 139 .........|.........|.........|.........|.........|.........|.........
 208 .........|.........|.........|.........|.........|.........|.........
 277 .........|.........|.........|.........|.........|.........|.........
 346 .........|.........|.........|.........|.........|.........|.........
 [...]
2026-10-10T16:47:48-01:00
4348 .........|.........|.........|.........|.........|.........|.........
4417 .........|.........|.........|.........|.........|.........|.........
4486 .........|.........|.........|.........|.........|.........|.........
4555 .........|.........|.........|.........|.........|.........|.........
[...]
.fi

At start time, the daemon prints a message and a date/time stamp. Then
it dumps a character every S seconds (S is the number of seconds it
sleeps between polls of the trigger device): see the dots in the above
example. In order to make it easy to count the heartbeat characters, a
different character (call it "group marker") is printed every X of
them: see the pipe character above. Moreover, a global heartbeat
counter is printed at the beginning of each line. Date/time stamps are
also printed periodically.

.TP
\fB-D "C"\fP
use character "C" as the heartbeat character (the dot in the above example)
.br
Default: "." (a dot)
.TP
\fB-g "G"\fP
use character "G" as the "group marker" character (the pipe character in
the above example)
.br
Default: "|" (the pipe character)
.TP
\fB-e EE\fP
EE (a positive integer) is the number of heartbeat characters after
which the "group marker" character is printed (the pipe character in
the above example)
.br
Default: 10
.TP
\fB-L LL\fP
LL (a positive integer) is the total number of heartbeat characters to
print on a single line; after printing LL heartbeat characters a new
line is started with the global counter and a new array of heartbeat
characters
.br
Default: 70
.TP
\fB-t TT\fP
TT (a positive integer) is the time interval, in seconds, between two
successive date/time stamps
.br
Default: 86400 (one day)

.P
Here is another (a bit unrealistic) example to further clarify the
effect of these options.
.br
This combination:
.br
      \fB-D "," -g "/" -e 3 -L 9 -t 27 -s 3\fP
.br
would print a heartbeat character every 3 seconds like this:

.nf
STARTING
2026-10-12T17:02:12-01:00
       1 ,,/,,/,,/
2026-10-12T17:02:39-01:00
      10 ,,/,,/,,/
2026-10-12T17:03:07-01:00
      19 ,,/,,/,,/
[...]
.fi

The deafult values for the heartbeat logging and the sleep time
(-s option) are such that LOG_FILE will grow by approximately
1.5MB per year.

.br
Other options:
.br
.TP
\fB-d\fP
(true/false) print debug messages.
.br
Default: false
.TP
\fB-V\fP
print version and exit
.TP
\fB-h\fP
print a help message and exit

.SS CLIENT OPTIONS

.TP
\fB-b\fP
with this option \fBshutdown-trigger\fP runs as a client and expects
another instance of the script already running as a daemon
.TP
\fB-C\fP
dump to stdout the current configuration, as resulting from the
command line options and configuration file, and exit
.TP
\fB-S\fP
dump the current state of the daemon. Note that this can differ from
the daemon configuration printed with \fB-C\fP option. For example,
the port used by the ncat command can have the default value of -1 in
the configuration, but it will have a value >=38000 in the daemon
state.
.TP
\fB-q\fP
tell the running daemon to quit. This is the proper way to stop the
daemon. When stopped this way, the daemon will perform housekeeping in
its runtime state directory; if brutally kille, it won't clean the
runtime state directory and this may cause troubles in a subsequent
run.
.TP
\fB-d\fP
print debug messages
.TP
\fB-V\fP
print version and exit
.TP
\fB-h\fP
print a help message and exit

